      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hill%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Settlers built a wooden blockhouse at Fort Hill between 1855 and 1856 to help contain regional conflicts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fort_hill_oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The blockhouse at Fort Hill later became part of Fort Yamhill before being moved to Dayton.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hill%2C_Oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early European-American settlement in Fort Hill accelerated during the mid-1850s due to a wave of pioneer migration.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fort_hill_oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pioneers in the Fort Hill area claimed public land under the Donation Land Act of 1850 to establish homesteads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fort_hill_oregon"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The summit of Fort Hill reaches about 600 feet, providing a subtle prominence in the undulating valley landscape.",
